<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Gold and implied Fed hike odds have moved inversely on a near daily basis since June 8, with the relationship strengthening sharply after the June 17 FOMC meeting. The shaded band marks the FOMC decision date.</p>
<h2>What This Means for the Conversation, Not Just the Position</h2>
<p>The behavioural research on the availability heuristic carries a specific implication here: correcting it requires naming the swap explicitly, not simply restating the facts about gold. Telling a client that gold fell 1.7% on Fed expectations does not address the deeper confusion, which is that their internal story for why they own the asset has quietly changed.</p>
<p>The more useful move is to ask the client directly what they believe gold is protecting them against this month, and to notice if the answer has shifted from their answer in March. If it has, that shift itself is the conversation, not a footnote to it.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who bought gold during the Hormuz crisis are confused rather than panicked. They expected gold to behave like insurance and are watching it behave like a risk asset instead, which feels like a breach of an implicit promise even though no promise was actually broken.</p>
<p>Clients without gold exposure who are simply watching the headlines feel a vague, secondhand anxiety: rates are going up, markets are falling, and gold, which they associate with safety, is falling too, which removes a mental anchor they were relying on without realising it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Gold is down about 7% over the past two weeks, and I want to walk through why, because the reason has actually changed since we last talked about it. In March, gold was reacting to the Strait of Hormuz crisis. That story is resolving well: oil is back under $70, tankers are moving again. What is pushing gold down now is something different, the market is pricing in a real chance the Fed raises rates again this year under the new chair. Higher rates make gold less attractive to hold because it does not pay interest. Both of those things can be true in the same month. Hormuz easing is good news for your overall portfolio. The Fed story is a separate variable, and it is the one actually moving gold right now. Your allocation was built to handle a range of outcomes, not just the one that was in the headlines in March.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who added gold tactically in March or April specifically as a Hormuz hedge and have not revisited the position since. They are most likely to misattribute the current move and make a reactive decision based on a story that no longer applies.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients holding gold as a strategic, long-term allocation of 5 to 10 percent. The recent volatility is noise relative to their time horizon, but they may still call with questions prompted by financial media coverage.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who have been waiting for a pullback to add to a gold position at a better entry point. The current dip is driven by a catalyst, Fed rate fear, that several major bank strategists view as having a high bar to fully realise.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull the entry date and original stated rationale for any client position in gold or gold-adjacent holdings (mining equities, bullion ETFs) added in March or April.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any client who has called twice in the past two weeks about gold specifically, this is a signal of rising anxiety that warrants a proactive call rather than waiting for a third call.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check whether the client''s gold allocation is sized as a true hedge (under 10 percent of the portfolio) or has grown disproportionately due to price appreciation since March, which changes the urgency of the conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversation noting which catalyst, geopolitical or monetary, the client identifies as their primary concern, for reference in the next call.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on gold and what actually changed<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our call. Gold has fallen about 7% over the past two weeks, and I wanted to put that in writing since the reason is a bit different from what drove gold earlier this year.<br><br>
Back in March, the Strait of Hormuz crisis pushed oil and gold higher together as a geopolitical hedge. That situation has been easing: oil is now back under $70 a barrel and tanker traffic through the strait has resumed. That part of the story is going the way we hoped.<br><br>
What is moving gold now is a separate development: markets are pricing in a real possibility the Federal Reserve raises rates again before year end. Higher rates make non-yielding assets like gold less attractive, which is the mechanical reason for the recent pullback.<br><br>
Nothing about your allocation requires a change based on this. I just wanted you to have the accurate picture rather than a headline-level one.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding gold ETFs or bullion:</strong> They bought into the Hormuz story without professional guidance and are now watching their position fall with no one to call and explain the shift in catalyst. This is a textbook gap between self-directed conviction and the ongoing analysis required to hold a position through a changing narrative.</p>
<p><strong>Clients of other advisors who have not heard from their advisor since the Fed dot plot:</strong> A gold drawdown of this size, on a catalyst this specific, is exactly the kind of event a disengaged advisor misses addressing proactively.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed gold has fallen sharply over the past two weeks, and I am guessing the reason being reported is not quite the full picture. Do you have a few minutes to talk through what is actually driving it?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor holding gold has a position but not a framework. They know the price went up in March and down in June, but without an advisor walking them through the change in catalyst, from geopolitical premium to monetary policy fear, they are left reacting to price alone. That is precisely the condition under which investors sell at the wrong time: not because the asset failed, but because the story attached to it became confusing and they resolved the confusion by exiting.</p>
<p>The advisor''s value here is not predicting where gold goes next. It is being the person who can explain, in real time, why the reason for a move has changed, which is the information a self-directed investor structurally cannot get without paying close, continuous attention to two entirely different markets, commodities and monetary policy, simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>What was your original reason for adding gold to your portfolio this year?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through why gold is falling now, given that the original reason you bought it seems to be resolving in a positive direction?</p>
<p>How do you currently decide when a price move in an asset like gold is something to act on versus something to ignore?</p>
<p>If gold fell another 5 percent from here, would you know whether that confirms or contradicts your original reason for holding it?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The reason gold is falling has changed, has your strategy?<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Gold fell sharply this week, its worst single session since June 10. If you are holding a position you added earlier this year during the Strait of Hormuz crisis, the reason behind this week''s move is actually quite different from the reason behind your original purchase.<br><br>
In March, gold rose alongside oil as a geopolitical hedge. That crisis is easing. What is pushing gold down now is a separate story: rising expectations of a Federal Reserve rate hike under the new chair.<br><br>
If you would find it useful to walk through what that shift means for your position, I would be glad to set up a short call.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
