var stepPath = "";
data.forEach(function(pt,i){
  var x = xp(i);
  var y = yr(pt.r);
  if (i===0) { stepPath += "M" + x + "," + y + " "; }
  else {
    var prevY = yr(data[i-1].r);
    stepPath += "L" + x + "," + prevY + " L" + x + "," + y + " ";
  }
});
svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:stepPath,fill:"none",stroke:"#4a5568","stroke-width":"2"}));
data.forEach(function(pt,i){
  svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:xp(i),cy:yr(pt.r),r:2.5,fill:"#4a5568"}));
});
for (var i=0;i<n;i++){
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:xp(i),y:H-margin.bottom+16,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"7.5","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#999999"}, data[i].q));
}
var lastX = xp(n-1), lastY = yr(data[n-1].r);
svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:lastX,cy:lastY,r:4,fill:"#4a5568"}));
var pillW = 46, pillH = 16;
var pillX = lastX - pillW - 6;
var pillY = lastY - pillH/2 - 14;
svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:pillW,height:pillH,fill:"#e8a825",rx:"3"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:pillX+pillW/2,y:pillY+pillH/2+4,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"9","font-weight":"700","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#111111"},"3%"));
svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:margin.left+10,y:margin.top+12,"text-anchor":"start","font-size":"7","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#888888"},"Rate hit 6% in early 2024, fell as BoC cut through 2025"));
if(_cs && _cs.parentNode) _cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Canada Revenue Agency prescribed interest rate announcements, Q1 2024 through Q3 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The rate is set quarterly from the prior quarter''s first-month average of 90-day Treasury bill yields, rounded up to the nearest whole point. Q4 2026 will be calculated from July yields and announced in September.</p>
<h2>The Planning Bridge</h2>
<p>The decision facing a client right now is not whether prescribed rate loans make sense in principle. It is timing. A client with investment income to split and a lower-income family member to lend to faces a real choice between locking in the known 3% rate today and waiting roughly three months to see whether the Q4 rate moves lower.</p>
<p>The honest answer for most clients is that the certainty of locking in now usually outweighs the uncertain benefit of waiting, particularly because the difference between a 3% and a possible 2% rate, while real, is small in absolute terms next to the cost of further delaying an income-splitting structure that compounds annually. The exception is a client already mid-decision who can reasonably wait ninety days without other consequences.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who have heard about falling bond yields and rising Fed hike odds in the same week feel pulled in two directions and are unsure which signal applies to their own tax planning decision. The prescribed rate''s quarterly lag is not intuitive, and clients may assume a rate change is more imminent or more remote than it actually is.</p>
<p>Clients already running a prescribed rate loan feel reassured by the five-quarter stability and may not realise their existing loan''s rate is locked regardless of what happens next, which means they need no action, only confirmation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate stays at 3% through September, the fifth quarter in a row at that level. If you already have a prescribed rate loan in place, nothing changes for you, the rate you locked in when you set it up is the rate for the life of that loan. If you have been considering setting one up, here is the actual decision in front of you: the rate that determines next quarter''s prescribed rate is being calculated right now, from this summer''s bond yields, and those yields have been falling as oil prices come down. That could mean a lower rate available in October. But it is not guaranteed, the Fed has been pulling in the opposite direction. My view is that for most income-splitting situations, locking in the known 3% rate today is worth more than waiting on a possible reduction that may not materialise.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Higher-net-worth clients and incorporated business owners considering a new prescribed rate loan for income splitting with a spouse, adult child, or family trust, where the loan principal is large enough that even small rate differences compound meaningfully.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with existing prescribed rate loans who need no action but may call after seeing rate-related headlines. A short reassurance call prevents an unnecessary visit.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who can comfortably wait until October without disrupting other plans, and who have a large enough loan amount that the potential rate difference justifies the delay.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with investment income to split and a lower-income spouse, adult child, or family trust who have not yet set up a prescribed rate loan.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review any existing prescribed rate loan documentation to confirm the locked-in rate and the January 30 annual interest payment deadline is on each client''s calendar.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag incorporated clients with shareholder loan structures who may be affected by the same Q4 prescribed rate calculation, separate from the family income-splitting use case.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar a check-in for early September, when the CRA typically announces the Q4 rate, to revisit any client who chose to wait.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Prescribed rate update and what it means for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our conversation. The CRA confirmed this week that the prescribed rate stays at 3% through September, unchanged for the fifth quarter running.<br><br>
If you already have a prescribed rate loan in place, this does not affect you. The rate is locked at the level in effect when the loan was made.<br><br>
If we have been discussing setting one up, the relevant detail is that the rate for the next quarter is calculated from this summer''s bond yields, which have been moving lower as oil prices ease. That creates a real possibility of a lower rate in October, though it is not guaranteed given competing pressure from the U.S. Federal Reserve''s recent rate signals.<br><br>
I am happy to walk through the numbers for your specific situation to help decide whether locking in now or waiting makes more sense.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Business owners and incorporated professionals with a lower-income spouse:</strong> A classic prescribed rate loan candidate who may not realise the strategy exists or may have been told it was not worth pursuing when rates were higher in 2024.</p>
<p><strong>Clients with adult children in lower tax brackets, particularly students or early-career professionals:</strong> Family income splitting through a prescribed rate loan is underused outside of high-net-worth circles, and a stable 3% rate makes the math attractive at almost any account size.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate stays at 3% for a fifth straight quarter, which is actually a meaningful planning window if you have investment income and a family member in a lower tax bracket. Do you have a few minutes to see if this applies to you?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A prospect managing their own investments rarely has a structure in place to split investment income with a lower-earning spouse or adult child, because the prescribed rate loan mechanism requires both tax knowledge and the discipline to maintain proper loan documentation and annual interest payments. Without that structure, all investment income is taxed in the higher earner''s hands every year, indefinitely.</p>
<p>The advisor''s role is to identify the opportunity, set up the loan correctly so it survives CRA scrutiny under the attribution rules, and time the decision against the prescribed rate cycle, including the live judgment call this quarter represents: lock in the known 3% now, or wait on a possible but uncertain Q4 reduction. A DIY investor has no mechanism for making that timing call well.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you or your spouse have investment income currently taxed in the higher earner''s name?</p>
<p>Have you ever looked into splitting investment income with a lower-income family member through a structured loan?</p>
<p>Do you know what the CRA prescribed rate is right now, or how it is calculated?</p>
<p>If a lower rate became available in a few months, would you know how that changes the decision to act now versus later?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A planning window most investors are not using<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate on family loans stays at 3% for a fifth straight quarter. If you have investment income and a spouse or adult child in a lower tax bracket, this rate is the basis for a structured strategy that can shift future investment income to their hands instead of yours, legally and durably.<br><br>
Most self-directed investors have never set this up, simply because it requires careful structuring and ongoing administration that falls outside typical DIY investing.<br><br>
If you would like to see whether this applies to your situation, I would be glad to walk through the numbers.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
