VALUES (
  '2026/06/25/tsx-energy-gold-fall-celestica-micron-rally',
  'market', 'article',
  'The TSX Fell on Oil and Gold Yesterday. Today It Has a Chip Problem to Watch Instead.',
  'Energy and gold names dragged the TSX down 0.5% Tuesday. A blowout Micron earnings report overnight has flipped the script for Canadian tech names heading into Thursday''s session.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX fell 0.5% to close at 34,736 Tuesday,</strong><span> as gold and energy names dragged the index lower while Canadian tech rebounded sharply.</span></li>
<li><strong>WTI crude settled at $70.34, its first close below $70 since March 2,</strong><span> pressuring Suncor, Cenovus, and Canadian Natural Resources.</span></li>
<li><strong>Gold fell to $4,040 on Fed rate-hike fear,</strong><span> dragging Agnico Eagle and Barrick lower even as the same disinflation story that hurt energy should have helped gold.</span></li>
<li><strong>Celestica rose 3.3% Tuesday ahead of Micron''s earnings,</strong><span> and Micron''s overnight blowout quarter has set up a sharply different open for Canadian semiconductor-adjacent names Thursday.</span></li>
<li><strong>The same morning, US core PCE inflation hit 3.4% year over year, the highest since October 2023,</strong><span> a hot but in-line print that markets are reading through in favour of the Micron-driven AI rally.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The S&P/TSX Composite fell 0.5% Tuesday to close at 34,736, pressured by weakness in commodity-linked sectors as the same de-escalation story that calmed energy markets also unsettled gold. Thursday''s session opens with a sharply different catalyst on the table: Micron''s blowout earnings overnight have reignited the AI trade globally, and the read-through for Canadian tech names is significant.</p>
<h2>What Actually Moved the TSX Tuesday</h2>
<p>WTI crude settled at $70.34 a barrel, down nearly 4% and its first close below $70 since March 2, as tanker traffic through the Strait of Hormuz continued recovering. Brent fell 4.3% to $73.74, its lowest level since before the conflict began on February 28. Canadian Natural dropped 3.7%, Suncor fell 3.4%, and Cenovus lost 4.1% on the move.</p>
<p>Gold told a different but related story. Spot gold fell 1.7% to $4,040 an ounce, its sharpest single-session loss since June 10, as Federal Reserve rate-hike expectations under Chair Kevin Warsh overwhelmed the same de-escalation that was easing oil prices. Agnico Eagle lost 4.1%, Barrick shed 4.3%, and Wheaton Precious Metals retreated 2.9%. The mechanism is distinct from energy''s: gold sold off on monetary policy fear even as the underlying geopolitical premium it had been carrying continued to unwind in the background.</p>
<p>Canadian technology stocks were the session''s clear outperformer, rebounding from the previous session''s global selloff. Shopify rose 6.1%, Constellation Software gained 2.8%, and Celestica climbed 3.3% ahead of Micron''s earnings report after the bell.</p>
<h2>The Overnight Catalyst That Changes Thursday''s Setup</h2>
<p>Micron reported fiscal third-quarter results that crushed expectations after Tuesday''s close. Revenue more than quadrupled from $9.3 billion a year earlier to $41.46 billion, beating analyst estimates of roughly $36 billion. Adjusted earnings per share came in at $25.11 against a $20.78 estimate. The company guided to approximately $50 billion in revenue for the current quarter.</p>
<p>The market reaction has been immediate and broad. Micron shares jumped roughly 18% in premarket trading. Qualcomm gained alongside it after raising its non-handset revenue guidance for fiscal 2029. Nasdaq 100 futures rose roughly 1.8 to 2.4% in early trading, with the rally extending into Asian markets, where South Korea''s Kospi surged as much as 6%.</p>
<p>For the TSX, the direct read-through runs through Celestica, the Toronto-listed electronics manufacturer that supplies into the same AI infrastructure and data centre buildout driving Micron''s demand surge. Celestica''s 3.3% gain Tuesday came before Micron reported, positioning the stock to extend that move on Thursday''s open rather than starting from a flat base. Constellation Software and Shopify, while less directly tied to AI memory demand specifically, both benefit from the same broad relief in technology sentiment.</p>
<h2>The Inflation Print the Market Chose to Look Past</h2>
<p>The Fed''s preferred inflation gauge complicated the picture without derailing it. Core PCE inflation rose to 3.4% year over year in May, the highest reading since October 2023, while headline PCE hit a 4.1% annual rate, the highest since April 2023. Both figures came in at or near consensus expectations rather than as a surprise.</p>
<p>In isolation, a 3.4% core reading would normally reinforce the Fed''s hawkish stance and pressure equities further. That it has not done so this morning is a function of timing: Micron''s results landed first and dominate sentiment heading into the open, while the in-line nature of the PCE print, however elevated in absolute terms, removed the risk of a hotter-than-expected surprise that could have forced a sharper repricing.</p>
<p>The combination leaves Thursday''s session with two genuinely separate stories running in parallel: a reignited AI trade lifting technology broadly, and a Fed that has one more data point supporting its hawkish dot plot, with the market choosing, for today, to weight the former more heavily.</p>
<p>The TSX has tracked a volatile but directionally consistent path lower over the past several sessions as commodity weakness offset intermittent tech strength.</p>
