<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Volume bars are illustrative relative session activity, shaded red on down days. The shaded band marks the June 17 FOMC decision under Chair Kevin Warsh.</p>
<h2>What to Watch at the Open</h2>
<p>The first thirty minutes of trading will show whether Celestica and the broader Canadian tech complex extend Tuesday''s gain on the Micron read-through, or whether the elevated core PCE print pulls rate-sensitive sectors back regardless of the chip rally. Energy and gold names are unlikely to reverse Tuesday''s losses meaningfully unless oil or the U.S. dollar moves sharply, neither of which the early data suggests.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy or gold exposure feel discouraged after a second consecutive rough session in those sectors and may be questioning whether to reduce exposure. Clients with technology exposure, conversely, feel a sharp reversal in sentiment from Tuesday morning to Thursday morning and may not understand why the mood shifted so quickly.</p>
<p>Clients tracking inflation headlines may also feel confused that a hot core PCE print did not produce a market selloff, since that disconnect runs against the simple "bad inflation news, bad market day" heuristic many investors carry.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Tuesday was a rough day for energy and gold names specifically, driven by two separate stories: oil falling on the Hormuz de-escalation, and gold falling on Fed rate-hike fear. Those are mechanical, well-understood moves, not a sign of broader market stress. Overnight, the picture shifted again. Micron, a major US memory chip maker, reported a blowout quarter that has reignited enthusiasm for AI-related stocks globally, and that is showing up directly in names like Celestica on the TSX. At the same time, US inflation data this morning came in hot but in line with expectations, which the market is treating as old news next to the Micron story. None of this changes your overall plan. It is useful context for why different parts of your portfolio are behaving differently this week.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated positions in Canadian energy or gold mining equities who have seen two consecutive down sessions in those names and may be anxious about further declines.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with diversified Canadian equity exposure through broad index funds, who are seeing offsetting moves between commodity weakness and tech strength that roughly net out at the index level.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients holding Celestica, Shopify, or other Canadian technology names directly, who are positioned to benefit from the Micron-driven sentiment shift heading into Thursday''s session.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios with concentrated energy or gold mining exposure for any rebalancing needs following two consecutive down sessions in those sectors.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients holding Celestica or other AI infrastructure-adjacent TSX names and prepare context on the Micron earnings read-through for any inbound questions.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the hot core PCE print for context in any client conversation referencing US inflation, distinguishing it from the in-line nature of the actual release.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor the opening thirty minutes of Thursday''s session for confirmation of whether the AI rally extends to Canadian names or fades.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
